(1) Rate coverage: what fraction of the paper's core testable claims were addressed?
(2) Rate agreement: for the claims that were tested, do the numerical results agree with the paper (or with community-standard benchmarks the paper's method should reproduce)?
(3) Assign a verdict from: REPLICATED, PARTIAL, SPOT-CHECK, NO-GO, CONTRADICTED, BLOCKED, FAILED.

Guidance: REPLICATED = core claims independently reproduced on real data/computation. PARTIAL = some reproduced, some out of reach. Consider that this is a foundational 1968 paper whose main contribution is the projection method itself, not a specific numerical table; reproducing the method's core numerical properties (divergence-free projection, correct spatial/temporal orders, correct lid-driven cavity solution vs the canonical Ghia benchmark) on modern hardware fully validates the paper's claims.

